text:
Model fallback — Model fallback
When a model is rate-limited, retired or down, the question moves to the next model in your list — and the one that answered goes first next time.
Give the panel more than one model and an outage stops being the reader's problem. A 429 from the first model sends the same question to the second; the model that answers is tried first on the next question, and the one that failed is set aside for a minute.

```ts
docPilot: {
  chat: {
    provider: 'openrouter',
    model: 'deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash',
    models: ['openai/gpt-4o-mini', 'google/gemini-2.5-flash'],
  },
}
```

Feedback — Feedback
Thumbs under each answer, four reasons and a comment on a thumbs-down — sent to your endpoint, or kept on the reader's device.
Two thumbs under every answer. A thumbs-down asks what went wrong — **Wrong answer**, **Incomplete**, **Not in the docs**, **Bad links** — and offers a comment box. Keys and tokens are stripped from the comment before it goes anywhere.

```ts
docPilot: {
  feedbackEndpoint: '/api/feedback', // null keeps votes on the reader's device
  feedback: {
    send: 'down',   // 'both' | 'up' | 'down' | 'none'
    comment: true,
  },
}
```

Conversation history — Conversation history
Past conversations stay on the reader's device, in localStorage. No account, no server, nothing sent anywhere.
The clock button in the panel header opens the reader's past conversations, newest first, titled by their first question and dated like *3 hours ago*. Press one to restore it; hover a row to delete it. Everything lives in the reader's browser.

```ts
docPilot: {
  history: {
    enabled: true,
    maxConversations: 20, // the oldest drops off past this
    exportThread: true,   // Copy conversation, in the header
    saveOnUnload: true,   // an answer cut off by a reload comes back
  },
}
```

Every language — Every language
The panel speaks the page's language, the model answers in the reader's, and greetings and key warnings ship in 18 languages.
Two languages matter, and DocPilot keeps them apart. The **panel's chrome** — buttons, headings, hints — follows the `lang` of the page it sits on. The **answer** follows the language the reader typed, whatever language your docs are written in.

```ts
docPilot: {
  i18n: {
    translations: { empty: { heading: 'How can I help?' } }, // every locale
    locales: {
      ru: { translations: { empty: { heading: 'Чем помочь?' }, composer: { placeholder: 'Спросите об {product}' } } },
      de: { translations: { empty: { heading: 'Wie kann ich helfen?' } } },
    },
  },
}
```

How this works — How this works
Show readers the exact instruction the model runs under, its tools and its scope — and optionally let them add their own.
An answer is easier to trust when the rules behind it are visible. With `prompt.show`, a **How this works** disclosure in the panel shows the system instruction, the tools the model may call, and what it is searching. With `prompt.allowAppend`, readers can add an instruction of their own.

```ts
docPilot: {
  prompt: {
    show: true,         // the "How this works" disclosure
    allowAppend: true,  // readers may add an instruction
    appendMaxChars: 500,
    extend: 'Prefer TypeScript in code samples.', // your addition, at build time
  },
}
```

Ask about a selection — Ask about a selection
Select a passage on the page or in an answer, press Ask AI, and the question arrives with that passage attached.
Select a sentence and a small **Ask AI** button appears above it. Press it: the panel opens with the passage attached to the composer as a removable quote, and the reader types the question about it.

```ts
docPilot: {
  quote: {
    fromAnswer: true, // select text inside an answer
    fromDocs: true,   // select text in the article itself
  },
  ui: { firstRunHint: true }, // "Select any passage to ask about it."
}
```

Static or server — Static or server
The same chat, run in one of two places — in the reader's browser against a static index, or on your server — what travels on each wire, what each install makes public, and how to choose.
A turn runs in one of two places. In the **static** install the reader's browser downloads the index and does the work; your server only attaches the model key. In the **server** install the browser sends the question and draws the stream; the index, the prompt and the key stay with you. The reader sees the same chat in both. Press an install to see what travels on each wire.

```ts
// static — the default: the index ships with the site
docPilot: { runtime: 'browser' }

// server — the turn runs on your server
docPilot: {
  runtime: 'server',
  server: { endpoint: '/ai/turn' },
}
```

Search scope — Search scope
The reader decides where the answer may come from — all docs, this page, this section, or a hand-picked set of pages.
The chip under the composer says what the next question searches. Press it and pick **All docs**, **This page**, **This section**, or any set of pages from the list. The hint line confirms it: *Searching this page.* Retrieval and citations both stay inside the scope.

```ts
docPilot: {
  scope: {
    enabled: true,        // the chip and the picker
    groupBySection: true, // the page list follows your sidebar groups
    filter: 'auto',       // a filter field once the list is long
  },
}
```

Panel shapes — Panel shapes
The same conversation in three shapes on one page — a question bar at the bottom centre, a popup above a floating button, and a drawer that pushes the page aside.
The panel has three shapes, and the conversation inside is the same in each: the thread, the sources, the scope, the history. Only where it sits on the page changes. The mock-up plays them in order — the bar, the popup, then the drawer that pushes the page.

```ts
// 1 — a question bar at the bottom centre, the shipped shape
docPilot: { ui: { panel: 'bar' } }

// 2 — a popup above the floating button
docPilot: { ui: { trigger: 'fab', panel: 'popup' } }

// 3 — a drawer that pushes the page aside
docPilot: { ui: { panel: 'drawer', layout: 'push', side: 'end' } }
```

Sources and passages — Sources and passages
Every claim carries a numbered citation. Open a source to read the exact passage the answer was drawn from.
Each claim in an answer ends with a number, and the **Sources** list under the answer says where each number points. With `citations.passage` on, every source row is also an accordion: the chevron opens the exact passage the model was given, inline, with no extra request.

```ts
docPilot: {
  citations: {
    passage: true,   // the accordion with the retrieved passage
    inCopy: true,    // copied answers end with a Sources: list
    pagesRead: true, // a refusal lists the pages it read
  },
}
```

Suggestions — Suggestions
Starter questions in the empty panel, prepared answers that need no model call, and follow-up questions under each answer.
An empty panel should not be a blank box. It shows a greeting and up to five questions to start from. `npx docpilot index` answers those questions at build time, so a click paints the answer in at once — no model call, no wait.

```ts
docPilot: {
  product: 'Acme',
  suggestions: {
    questions: ['How do I get started?', 'How do I authenticate requests?', 'What does it cost?'],
    followUps: true, // 2–3 next questions under the newest answer
  },
}
```

Tools and actions — Tools and actions
Give the panel your own tools — live data the answer cites, and next steps the reader runs with one press. Off by default.
Your documentation answers what is written down. Tools answer what is *live* — a plan's limits, an API's status, the reader's own account — and actions take the *next step*: open the pricing calculator, write to support, book a demo. Both are off until you add a `tools` key, and without one every request is the one DocPilot always sent.

```ts
docPilot: {
  tools: {
    plan_limits: {
      description: 'Live limits of one plan: requests per day, seats and storage.',
      parameters: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: { plan: { type: 'string', enum: ['free', 'pro', 'team'] } },
        required: ['plan'],
      },
      run: { fetch: '/api/plans/{plan}' },
    },
    contact_support: {
      title: 'Contact support',
      label: 'Write to support',
      description: 'Send the question to the support team when the docs do not answer it.',
      parameters: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          email: { type: 'string', format: 'email', description: 'Where support replies' },
          message: { type: 'string', format: 'multiline', description: 'The problem' },
        },
        required: ['email', 'message'],
      },
      confirm: 'form',
      run: { fetch: '/api/support', method: 'POST' },
    },
  },
}
```

Tools and actions — What runs a tool
DocPilot asks three places, in this order:

1. **A handler you register in the page.** It wins over the config, so you can replace a `fetch` with code without touching the definition.
2. **A function in the config.** This works only on a server install, where the config is never sent to a browser.
3. **The declarative `run`:**
 - `{fetch: '/api/plans/{plan}'}`. Each `{name}` is replaced by that argument, URL-encoded. A placeholder goes in the path or the query, never in the scheme or the host, and an argument that would make a `.` or `..` path segment fails the call before anything is sent. A `GET` sends nothing else. A `POST` sends `{tool, args, context: {question, page}}` as JSON. A JSON reply is parsed, and any other reply is read as text.
 - `{href: '/pricing?plan={plan}'}`. A link to your own host opens in place. A link to another host opens in a new tab without `window.opener`.
 - `{event: 'acme:open-demo'}`. DocPilot dispatches a `CustomEvent` on `window` with `{tool, args, context}` in `detail`. This is the way to open **your own** modal or form.

```js [docs/.vitepress/theme/index.js]
import DefaultTheme from 'vitepress/theme'
import { withDocPilot, registerTool } from '@cloflin/docpilot/theme'

if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
  registerTool('plan_limits', async ({ plan }, { signal }) => {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/plans/${plan}`, { signal })
    return res.json()
  })
  window.addEventListener('acme:open-demo', (e) => openDemoModal(e.detail.args))
}

export default withDocPilot(DefaultTheme)
```

Triggers and layout — Triggers and layout
Four ways into the panel — a question bar, a navbar icon, a keyboard shortcut and a link under the article — and three shapes it opens in.
A reader opens the panel from the question bar at the bottom of the page, from an icon beside your search box, with ⌘ I (Ctrl I), or from the *Didn't find it? Ask DocPilot* link under each article. Press a variant to see where it puts the door.

```ts
docPilot: {
  ui: {
    trigger: 'fab',      // 'nav' | 'screen' | 'fab' | 'both' | 'none' | an array
    panel: 'auto',       // 'bar' with the floating placement, else 'drawer'; or 'popup'
    side: 'end',         // 'left' | 'right' | 'start' | 'end'
    fabLabel: 'Ask AI',  // false for an icon-only button
  },
}
```
